    title: 'What considerations go into plotting time series charts?',
    summary: 'Time series charts plot values against a datetime axis, and effective ones account for resampling frequency, trend/seasonality, and multiple series alignment.',
    explanation: 'Plotting time series data requires a proper datetime index or x-axis so that spacing between points reflects actual elapsed time rather than row order, which matters when data has irregular timestamps. Resampling to a consistent frequency (daily, weekly, monthly) before plotting often clarifies trend and seasonality by smoothing out noise. Overlaying rolling averages or using twin axes for series with very different scales are common techniques, and clear axis labeling/date formatting is essential for readability. Multiple series should share aligned time ranges to allow meaningful comparison.',
    code: "import pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\ndates = pd.date_range('2023-01-01', periods=365, freq='D')\nvalues = pd.Series(range(365), index=dates) + (pd.Series(range(365)) % 30) * 5\n\nmonthly = values.resample('ME').mean()\nrolling_avg = values.rolling(window=7).mean()\n\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(10, 4))\nax.plot(values.index, values, alpha=0.3, label='Daily')\nax.plot(rolling_avg.index, rolling_avg, label='7-day rolling avg')\nax.plot(monthly.index, monthly, label='Monthly avg', linewidth=2)\nax.legend()\nplt.show()",
    interviewQuestion: 'Why might resampling a noisy daily time series to weekly or monthly frequency make trends easier to interpret?',
