    title: 'What feature engineering techniques improve data analysis, not just modeling?',
    summary: 'Feature engineering for analysis includes binning continuous variables, creating ratio/interaction features, and extracting date parts to reveal patterns not visible in raw columns.',
    explanation: 'Even outside of predictive modeling, engineered features often make patterns easier to analyze: binning a continuous variable like age into groups can reveal non-linear trends that a scatter plot misses, and ratio features (e.g. revenue per customer) normalize for scale differences across groups. Extracting components from datetime columns, such as day-of-week or is_weekend flags, frequently uncovers cyclical patterns that raw timestamps hide. One-hot or frequency encoding of categorical variables can also make it possible to compute correlations or run groupby aggregations that would otherwise be impossible on raw text categories. The key discipline is deriving features that are interpretable and directly answer an analysis question, rather than engineering purely for model performance.',
    code: "import pandas as pd\n\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\n    'order_date': pd.to_datetime(['2024-01-06', '2024-01-08', '2024-01-13']),\n    'revenue': [500, 1200, 300],\n    'customers': [10, 20, 5]\n})\n\ndf['is_weekend'] = df['order_date'].dt.dayofweek >= 5\ndf['revenue_per_customer'] = df['revenue'] / df['customers']\n\nage_bins = pd.cut([22, 35, 41, 60, 19], bins=[0, 25, 40, 60, 100],\n                   labels=['18-25', '26-40', '41-60', '60+'])\n\nprint(df)\nprint(age_bins)",
    interviewQuestion: 'Give an example of a derived feature that would make a trend visible in EDA that the raw columns would hide.',
